course: adult-life
lesson: 2
title: Shifts, nights and other ways of working
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Not every grown-up works nine to five in an office; many work shifts, nights, part-time, for themselves, or in two jobs at once.
explain: Shops, hospitals, buses and factories need people at all hours, so their workers follow a rota that can change every week.
explain: Picture a house in Cork at lunchtime on a Saturday: the kids tiptoe past the bedroom door, whispering "Shh, Dad's on nights", because he drove a delivery lorry until six this morning.
explain: How someone works decides when they sleep, when they are home, how sure the money is, and what comes off their pay before it reaches the bank.
explain: Knowing this helps you understand why a family's week is planned round a piece of paper on the fridge.
fact: A rota shows someone's shifts, set blocks of working hours that can change every week, so family plans have to fit round it.
fact: Night shifts mean working through the night and sleeping in the day; nurses, drivers, bakers and warehouse workers often do them.
fact: Sleeping in daylight is hard, so quiet in the house matters a lot when someone is on nights.
fact: Overtime is working extra hours beyond the normal ones; sometimes it is paid at a higher rate, sometimes not paid at all.
fact: Self-employed people work for themselves, like many plumbers and hairdressers; they choose their hours but get no sick pay or holiday pay and must work out and pay their own tax.
fact: On a zero-hours contract the employer promises no set number of hours, so the hours and the pay can change every week.
fact: When someone is too ill to work they call in sick; some employers keep paying them, some pay only a little, and the self-employed get nothing.
fact: Some people go to work ill because they can't afford to lose the pay, which spreads germs to everyone else.
fact: Annual leave is paid holiday from work; most full-time workers get a few weeks of it a year, bank holidays included.
fact: Annual leave must be agreed with the boss so that not everyone is off at once, which is why summer holidays get booked early.
fact: A payslip shows what someone earned and what came off before the money reached the bank, mostly tax, which pays for schools, hospitals and roads.
fact: Gross pay is the big number at the top of the payslip; net or take-home pay is what is actually left to spend.
fact: Part-time means working fewer hours than a full week, for example to look after children, to study, or for health; it means less pay but more time.
fact: Some people take a second job because low pay or rising prices make one job not enough, which leaves very little time to rest.
exercise: Mum's rota on the fridge says "Early, early, late, off, off." What is a rota? | A list of her shifts, which can change every week | A list of chores for the children to do before school each day | A shopping list for the week | A list of her colleagues' birthdays
exercise: Why do shops, hospitals and buses need shift workers? | They need people at all hours, not just in the daytime | Their bosses like to keep workers confused and tired all the time | Shift workers are paid less than everyone else | It is the law that every job has shifts
exercise: A whisper in the hall at lunchtime: "Shh, Dad's on nights." What does it mean? | Dad worked through the night and is sleeping now | Dad is watching a scary film about the night in the front room | Dad goes out with friends every night | Dad is afraid of the dark
exercise: Dad is on nights. When is it right to wake him? | Only in a real emergency | Whenever you want a snack | When your friend rings the doorbell to ask you out to play | When the telly gets boring
exercise: "I'm doing overtime this weekend." What is overtime? | Working extra hours beyond the normal ones | Working fewer hours than usual because the shop is quiet | A sport played at the weekend | Taking time off to rest
exercise: Is overtime always paid extra? | No, sometimes it is paid more and sometimes not at all | Yes, it is always paid double, by law, in every job there is | Yes, but only at weekends | No, overtime is never paid
exercise: "Being your own boss is great until the tax return." What kind of worker says this? | A self-employed person who must sort out their own tax | A worker who has just been made a manager at a big firm | Somebody who is retired | A child who is in charge of their own pocket money
exercise: What does a self-employed plumber NOT get? | Sick pay and holiday pay | Customers who pay for the work | The right to choose their hours | Money for jobs they finish
exercise: "They've only given me eight hours this week." What kind of contract is this person probably on? | A zero-hours contract, with no set hours | A salary paid the same every month | A contract for exactly eight hours every week forever | A self-employed contract with their own customers
exercise: Mum croaks on the phone: "I'm calling in sick." What is she doing? | Telling her employer she is too ill to work | Phoning a doctor to come round to the house straight away | Ringing a friend to have a moan | Ordering medicine by phone
exercise: Why do some people go to work when they are ill? | They can't afford to lose the pay | Being ill at work makes them get better faster | The law forbids staying at home | Their germs cannot spread at work
exercise: "I need to book my annual leave before it runs out." What is annual leave? | Paid holiday from work | Time when the leaves fall off trees | A yearly trip the office takes together | Unpaid time off after an illness
exercise: Why are summer holidays booked early? | Leave must be agreed so not everyone is off at once | Holidays get more expensive with every single hour you wait | The boss chooses holidays by lottery | Leave is only allowed in winter
exercise: Dad frowns at a slip of paper: "Look how much they've taken off." What is the paper? | His payslip, showing pay and what came off it | A bill for something he broke at work last month | A receipt from the supermarket | A letter about a parking fine
exercise: On a payslip, which number is what Dad can actually spend? | Net, or take-home, pay | Gross pay | The tax code | The total of all the deductions
exercise: "I only work part-time." What does it mean? | Working fewer hours than a full week | Working only part of the year in another country | Doing only part of the job badly | Working in a toy shop
exercise: "Your uncle's got two jobs now." What is the most likely reason? | One job does not pay enough to live on | He finds one job too easy and gets bored at work | His boss told him to get another | Two jobs give him more time to rest
test: Overheard: "Can't do Sam's party Sat, I'm on lates." Why can't she go? | Her rota has her working that evening | She is always late for parties and doesn't like it | She doesn't like Sam | She has to sleep all day Saturday
test: Your cousin says: "My mum sleeps all day, she's just lazy." Put him right. | She works night shifts and must sleep in the day | He is right, sleeping in the day is lazy and always will be | She must be ill and should see a doctor | She sleeps because she has no job
test: Overheard: "Unpaid overtime again, they take the piss." What is he angry about? | Working extra hours and not being paid for them | Being paid far too much money for the work he does | Being sent home early without pay | Having to work part-time
test: Grandad: "Your aunt went self-employed, so no sick pay, she worked through the flu." Why did she work with the flu? | If she doesn't work, she doesn't get paid | Her boss made her come in | Work is the best cure for flu | She had run out of holiday
test: Mum, quietly: "Only 12 hours next week. Dunno how we'll pay the gas." Why is money suddenly tight? | Her hours, and so her pay, went down this week | The gas company doubled its prices overnight for everybody | She got a pay rise and must pay more tax | She is taking annual leave
test: Your sister says: "Tax and stuff on a payslip just goes in the bin." Put her right. | The tax pays for schools, hospitals and roads | She is right, it is thrown away by the office every month | It goes to the boss as a bonus | It is given back at Christmas
test: Dad: "Gross is three grand but I only see two and a bit." What is the "two and a bit"? | His net, or take-home, pay | His gross pay | His tax | His overtime
test: Your friend says: "My mum only works mornings, so she doesn't really work." Put her right. | Part-time work is real work, just fewer hours | She is right, only full-time is real work | Morning work doesn't count as a job | Her mum is retired
test: Overheard: "He's on the buses by day and Deliveroo till midnight, poor sod." What does that tell you? | He has two jobs and very little rest | He is self-employed and chooses easy hours | He is on a zero-hours contract with no work | He is on annual leave from both jobs
test: Mum: "Booked my two weeks for August, before Karen grabs them!" Why the hurry? | Not everyone can be off at the same time | Holiday disappears if you don't book it by Monday morning | Karen will be her boss in August | August leave is unpaid
test: Mum, croaky: "Cough's awful but if I don't go in I don't get paid." What follows if she goes in ill? | Her germs may spread to her colleagues | She will get better faster | She will be paid double | Her boss will give her a prize
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises a zero-hours contract? | No minimum number of working hours is guaranteed by the employer | An arrangement whereby the employee works no hours whatsoever and is paid in full | An arrangement whereby the employee works exclusively at night | An arrangement whereby wages are paid only once per annum
exam: Identify the principal disadvantage of self-employment in respect of illness. | No sick pay is received during any period of incapacity | The self-employed person is forbidden by statute to fall ill | The government pays the self-employed double when ill | Illness automatically terminates self-employment
exam: Given that a payslip records gross pay and deductions, ascertain which figure represents the sum available for expenditure. | Net pay, being gross pay less all deductions | Gross pay, prior to any deductions | The aggregate of income tax and National Insurance | The pension contribution alone
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning annual leave. | It is paid holiday, to be agreed with the employer so that not all are absent at once | Annual leave is granted solely at the employer's whim and is invariably unpaid | Annual leave may be taken only by part-time workers | Annual leave is restricted to the month of August alone
